LINES = {
 's1': "Kalita. Follow the wallets that actually beat the market — on Robinhood Chain.",
 's2': "Copy-trading is a “who to follow” problem, and the market answers it dishonestly. Smart-wallet lists are built by hindsight and never audited. A popular list of eleven hundred wallets, measured over thirty days: twenty-three point seven percent of its signals doubled within an hour, against twenty-one point four expected at the same curve positions. Nobody who follows it has ever seen that number.",
 's3': "Kalita scores every early buyer on the Pons bonding curve — eight million signals — and keeps a live, self-correcting list.",
 's4': "Three ideas. Each wallet is measured as a follower entering two seconds later. It is compared with buyers at the same position on the curve, because early is not the same as skilled. And every estimate is pessimistic and validated out of sample, next to a placebo list.",
 's5': "Candidates with their edge over the market, filters, a change log, personal lists, export to a copy-trading engine and an API — in twelve languages. Sign in with a wallet or an email.",
 's6': "Validation catches luck: a wallet at thirty-eight percent on seven hundred and ninety-one signals fell to fifteen percent on the week it never saw. It is not on the list.",
 's7': "Every daily list is sealed on chain in Kalita Registry as a Merkle root — no contract owner, no admin key, no upgrades. Anyone can verify who was on the list on any day, and measure it only on trades that came after. Fifteen Foundry tests.",
 's8': "Thirty days, out of sample: the top-hundred list hit two-x within an hour twenty-one point eight percent of the time, against fourteen point eight for the market. Placebo lists stay at the market; skill persists week to week. Five hypotheses, verdicts published — the negative ones too.",
 's9': "kalita dot tech. Who to follow — with a record that cannot be rewritten.",
}
